**Explanation:** The key concepts in this question are related to the specific heats and degrees of freedom of the gases involved. 

For monoatomic gases, like hydrogen, the heat capacity (at constant volume) \( C_V \) is higher than for diatomic gases, like ammonia. Hydrogen has a higher specific heat compared to ammonia because it requires more energy to increase its temperature. Thus, if it took 5J to increase the temperature of the hydrogen, it would take less heat (in indirect relationship due to their type of degrees of freedom) for ammonia to reach the same increase in temperature.

