To find the direction of the aircraft's velocity with respect to the ground, we can use vector addition. The velocity of the aircraft relative to the air and the velocity of the wind are both vectors.

1. The aircraft's airspeed: 200 km/h (unknown direction)
2. The wind speed: 56 km/h (from west to east, hence positive in the eastward direction).

Let the eastward direction be positive along the x-axis and the northward direction be positive along the y-axis.

The horizontal (x) and vertical (y) components based on the coordinates can be expressed as:

- The aircraft needs to counterbalance the effect of the wind to achieve a resultant velocity measured as 192 km/h.
- Its ground speed vector can be expressed as:
   - \( v_{x} = v_{\text{aircraft},x} + v_{\text{wind},x} \)
   - The speed acts on the x-axis -> \( v_{\text{aircraft},x} - 56 \, \text{km/h} = 192 \, \text{km/h} \)

So substituted and solved: 
\[ v_{\text{aircraft},x} = 192\, \text{km/h} + 56\, \text{km/h} \] 
\[ v_{\text{aircraft},x} = 248\, \text{km/h} \] in the eastward direction.

